[SPEAKER_11]: It's different working with volunteers who may or may not do what you want, and an intern who you say do X and they better do it or they get a bad review.

779
01:16:05,307 --> 01:16:17,031
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm also really curious to see, even if we did get an intern, how could we get somebody from the CCSR working collaboratively with the intern, right?

780
01:16:17,731 --> 01:16:23,313
[SPEAKER_08]: And do the students from the CCSR, can they get some sort of credit for this?

781
01:16:24,292 --> 01:16:29,773
[SPEAKER_08]: really important project work because they do it.

782
01:16:30,153 --> 01:16:32,754
[SPEAKER_01]: They do a capstone type project every year.

783
01:16:33,394 --> 01:16:37,535
[SPEAKER_01]: They break out into small groups and they do projects every year.

784
01:16:37,555 --> 01:16:42,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So it might be something that we really want to think on approaching the academic year.

785
01:16:43,576 --> 01:16:43,816
[SPEAKER_08]: Yes.

786
01:16:43,996 --> 01:16:45,477
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah, I agree, Heather.

787
01:16:46,697 --> 01:16:47,057
[SPEAKER_08]: I agree.

788
01:16:48,670 --> 01:17:01,939
[SPEAKER_08]: And then that satisfies my own curiosity, is how do you get younger people interested in local government, in being civic-minded, right?

789
01:17:01,959 --> 01:17:04,041
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, that's great.

790
01:17:04,081 --> 01:17:05,202
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you for bringing that up.

791
01:17:06,254 --> 01:17:16,539
[SPEAKER_00]: I was going to say yes to, I think that, especially having younger students do you know interning and different research projects is a great idea.

792
01:17:17,099 --> 01:17:23,242
[SPEAKER_00]: But I know that Marcia if you were interested in legal research specifically like with ADA requirements and things like that.

793
01:17:24,223 --> 01:17:28,785
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of the law schools in the area have either health law clinics or disability law clinics.

794
01:17:29,685 --> 01:17:35,927
[SPEAKER_00]: And a lot of times people, once they fulfill their health law clinics, they're looking to do similar work.

795
01:17:36,507 --> 01:17:46,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So if we're looking for like specific legal issues to be researched and things like that, I'd like, I know Suffolk has a health law clinic.

796
01:17:47,730 --> 01:17:51,711
[SPEAKER_00]: And also those will work for free.

797
01:17:52,252 --> 01:17:56,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So if funding was an issue, I probably- You can have free interns.

798
01:17:58,283 --> 01:18:08,267
[SPEAKER_11]: Then the responsibility is to fill out an evaluation, probably, if it's for a school.

799
01:18:09,647 --> 01:18:10,027
[SPEAKER_08]: It's harder.

800
01:18:13,260 --> 01:18:15,021
[SPEAKER_08]: Work-study is different.

801
01:18:16,142 --> 01:18:18,022
[SPEAKER_08]: Work-study, we don't have to pay.

802
01:18:18,383 --> 01:18:32,310
[SPEAKER_08]: But I don't think we should worry about the financial mechanism at this time, because this is too solid of an idea to let the monies become the barrier.

803
01:18:33,250 --> 01:18:41,794
[SPEAKER_08]: If this is what it's going to take to help the commission move forward, because again, this is not a commission where there is a stipend attached.

804
01:18:43,771 --> 01:18:57,298
[SPEAKER_08]: you know, Gabrielle, you can, I don't even know, you know, if you're working, but you can like, let's say, leave your job to go join a Zoom on our behalf, you know, it's not a stipend of that manner.

805
01:18:57,498 --> 01:19:00,340
[SPEAKER_08]: So let's, the APA funds are there.

806
01:19:00,400 --> 01:19:03,001
[SPEAKER_08]: This is, I feel like this is really valid.

807
01:19:03,101 --> 01:19:04,982
[SPEAKER_08]: I feel like this is really important.

808
01:19:07,366 --> 01:19:16,570
[SPEAKER_08]: And I think that sometimes, you know, when people talk about, you know, all the different abilities, folks don't realize just how complicated it really is.

809
01:19:17,930 --> 01:19:20,712
[SPEAKER_08]: You know, it's complicated.

810
01:19:20,732 --> 01:19:24,253
[SPEAKER_08]: You know, somebody said to me, why are you guys just doing the signs?

811
01:19:24,353 --> 01:19:31,116
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm like, because we don't have a billion dollars to tear the building down, bring the building back up, you know, change the roads.

812
01:19:33,157 --> 01:19:33,297
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah.

813
01:19:34,409 --> 01:19:37,733
[SPEAKER_08]: So we want to start with something.

814
01:19:37,753 --> 01:19:49,046
[SPEAKER_06]: I think when I put the letter together, I will I'm taking notes and will include what I can in there.

815
01:19:49,747 --> 01:19:52,150
[SPEAKER_06]: And then I think it will be important to decide or.

816
01:19:54,005 --> 01:19:58,447
[SPEAKER_06]: to share your input on what we want the intern to do or multiple people, right?

817
01:19:58,487 --> 01:20:04,811
[SPEAKER_06]: If it's not, if we have different types of people helping out and then we can kind of farm out or divide out those responsibilities.

818
01:20:07,972 --> 01:20:18,318
[SPEAKER_11]: I think it's easier to have one person to start this process because every volunteer that you get has to be

819
01:20:19,520 --> 01:20:23,842
[SPEAKER_11]: right into supervised, right into the goals.

820
01:20:23,942 --> 01:20:28,984
[SPEAKER_11]: It's not the same as saying, you know, you go to the library and find out this law.

821
01:20:31,385 --> 01:20:42,309
[SPEAKER_11]: So I guess the reason I suggested that, I wasn't aware of yours, Gabby, but I know the high school one, and I think it's great.

822
01:20:43,189 --> 01:20:47,571
[SPEAKER_11]: But I also know what it takes to coordinate volunteers because I've done it.

823
01:20:49,285 --> 01:20:51,467
[SPEAKER_11]: Well, that's a full time job.

824
01:20:51,988 --> 01:20:56,793
[SPEAKER_11]: If you have a lot of volunteers and projects that are happening.

825
01:20:59,416 --> 01:21:04,281
[SPEAKER_11]: And I know you're split so many ways is going to do that.

826
01:21:07,799 --> 01:21:11,942
[SPEAKER_10]: There's lots of me around.

827
01:21:13,984 --> 01:21:16,807
[SPEAKER_10]: I come in many different forms.

828
01:21:17,467 --> 01:21:23,372
[SPEAKER_08]: But you know, this is actually timely because I'm also going to be relocating my office.

829
01:21:25,434 --> 01:21:41,263
[SPEAKER_08]: The reality is that I'm on the second floor and as much as I love being in the same suite as the mayor, it is not conducive to the role and it's not the most barrier free location.

830
01:21:41,823 --> 01:21:52,651
[SPEAKER_08]: So if I was to have somebody come in, it would be difficult for a person to maneuver if they were using any sort of mobility device.

831
01:21:53,111 --> 01:22:03,038
[SPEAKER_08]: There's no space for me to put a table if somebody preferred to transfer out of a chair and sit comfortably at a table.

832
01:22:03,798 --> 01:22:09,042
[SPEAKER_08]: I don't have a table where a person can even pull their chair in and be comfortable, you know?

833
01:22:10,623 --> 01:22:18,449
[SPEAKER_08]: I will be relocating to the first floor that has a much, much better and better setup.

834
01:22:19,430 --> 01:22:23,954
[SPEAKER_08]: And I'm, you know, also if somebody had a visual impairment and use the cane.

835
01:22:24,908 --> 01:22:30,835
[SPEAKER_08]: it would be difficult, not to mention, you know, some of the other things like other aspects of my role.

836
01:22:31,936 --> 01:22:36,801
[SPEAKER_08]: But, you know, Marsha, I really, really am grateful for you starting that.

837
01:22:37,482 --> 01:22:43,169
[SPEAKER_08]: And I do see that we could, I see that there's a way that we could have an intern

838
01:22:45,680 --> 01:23:09,870
[SPEAKER_08]: leads the project, right, because interns typically are for lengthy periods of time, they're not for two months, and I also see how we could weave in the CCSR students, granted they're more time limited, agree with what Heather said, get it, you know, saying at the start of the academic year,

839
01:23:10,450 --> 01:23:15,491
[SPEAKER_08]: we are going to have some of these opportunities, you know, so that at least this way the students know.

840
01:23:16,371 --> 01:23:32,154
[SPEAKER_08]: And then if we have a, we run into a situation and we need like some legal research or some specific ADA research, we could reach out to the legal clinics and see, hey.

841
01:23:32,754 --> 01:23:35,054
[SPEAKER_11]: I wasn't disagreeing with you in any way.

842
01:23:35,074 --> 01:23:37,395
[SPEAKER_11]: I understand the value of it.

843
01:23:38,592 --> 01:23:45,498
[SPEAKER_11]: to understand from having done it, that it requires a lot of time of a leader.

844
01:23:46,078 --> 01:23:51,943
[SPEAKER_11]: You're pulling in people who are very busy with other things.

845
01:23:52,143 --> 01:23:53,905
[SPEAKER_11]: It's not the only thing in their life.

846
01:23:54,926 --> 01:24:06,075
[SPEAKER_11]: And it's not necessarily a full-time job, but it will take, cut into your time or someone's time.

847
01:24:11,134 --> 01:24:14,878
[SPEAKER_08]: Commissioners, we are at 7.02.

848
01:24:18,101 --> 01:24:25,348
[SPEAKER_08]: Susan, I think this is where we ask for this public participation is next on the list.

849
01:24:26,589 --> 01:24:27,750
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay.

850
01:24:29,151 --> 01:24:33,815
[SPEAKER_12]: I don't see any public, but I am not sure.

851
01:24:33,835 --> 01:24:36,278
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah, I don't see any either.

852
01:24:37,888 --> 01:24:42,770
[SPEAKER_12]: So I think we can move on.

853
01:24:46,592 --> 01:24:49,173
[SPEAKER_08]: Correspondence and announcements.

854
01:24:51,554 --> 01:24:53,615
[SPEAKER_02]: OK.

855
01:24:53,815 --> 01:24:56,276
[SPEAKER_02]: Any correspondence or announcements?

856
01:25:02,239 --> 01:25:02,419
[SPEAKER_09]: No?

857
01:25:02,479 --> 01:25:04,140
[SPEAKER_09]: Other than the announcement that

858
01:25:05,127 --> 01:25:09,950
[SPEAKER_09]: There is now an official Google doc for our vision, our goals.

859
01:25:09,990 --> 01:25:11,010
[SPEAKER_09]: I think I called them goals.

860
01:25:11,190 --> 01:25:14,112
[SPEAKER_09]: And at least two other people are in there editing it.

861
01:25:14,192 --> 01:25:17,033
[SPEAKER_09]: So yay, we have new notes.

862
01:25:17,053 --> 01:25:18,094
[SPEAKER_08]: Yay.

863
01:25:18,114 --> 01:25:19,735
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you, thank you, thank you.

864
01:25:19,775 --> 01:25:20,475
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you.

865
01:25:20,495 --> 01:25:20,575
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay.

866
01:25:22,056 --> 01:25:26,738
[SPEAKER_02]: And now are we at an end?

867
01:25:31,458 --> 01:25:38,040
[SPEAKER_08]: A huge thank you to you, Susan, before we end, for serving as chair.

868
01:25:38,760 --> 01:25:44,602
[SPEAKER_08]: Welcome, welcome, welcome, Tom, Linda, and Megan.

869
01:25:45,202 --> 01:25:50,083
[SPEAKER_08]: And thank you to all the other commissioners for continuing to serve.

870
01:25:51,163 --> 01:25:55,345
[SPEAKER_08]: Please don't forget, no meeting next month because you're all on holiday.

871
01:25:55,365 --> 01:25:55,465
[SPEAKER_08]: Enjoy.

872
